  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. I haven't opened the dashboard to check how the panels render. The rule's status is still \"pending\" because it hasn't run yet.\n\n**What the data holds:** Kibana Sample Data Logs has web server requests with a `timestamp`. Each request has a response code (`response`), a file type (`extension`), the bytes transferred (`bytes`), the client IP, a source and destination location (`geo.src` and `geo.dest`), and the user's OS and browser (`machine.os` and `agent`).\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-5831752e Web logs overview\" has four panels:\n- Total requests\n- Requests over time\n- Requests by response code (a pie chart)\n- Average bytes by extension (a bar chart)\n\nIt is at http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-5831752e-web-logs-overview-90635d11bb54\n\n**Rule:** \"EFF-5831752e 5xx spike\" is an Elasticsearch query rule that runs every minute. It counts documents in `kibana_sample_data_logs` where `response.keyword` matches `5[0-9][0-9]`, and it fires when that count is above 10 in the last 5 minutes. I used the keyword field because `response` is a text field. That query matches 441 documents across the whole index, so the pattern works. The rule has no action attached, so when it fires it will show up in Kibana but won't send a notification.",
